ing Generalized Expectation-Maximization Algorithm ... Fessler and Alfred O. ... This paper describes the space-alternating generalized EM (SAGE) method, which updates the parameters *sequentially* by alternating between several small *hidden-data spaces* defined by the algorithm designer. ... Several approaches to this problem have been proposed,\nSpace-Alternating Generalized Expectation-Maximization Algorithm\n\nJeffrey A. Fessler and Alfred O. Hero  \nDept. of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science  \nThe University of Michigan  \nEmail: fessler@umich.edu\n\nIEEE T-SP 42(10): 2664-77, Oct. 1994\n\n**ABSTRACT**\n\nThe expectation-maximization (EM) method can facilitate maximizing likelihood functions that arise in statistical estimation problems. In the classical EM paradigm, one iteratively maximizes the conditional log-likelihood of a single unobservable *complete data space*, rather than maximizing the intractable likelihood function for the measured or incomplete data. EM algorithms update all parameters *simultaneously*, which has two drawbacks: 1) slow convergence, and 2) difficult maximization steps due to coupling when smoothness penalties are used.\n\nThis paper describes the space-alternating generalized EM (SAGE) method, which updates the parameters *sequentially* by alternating between several small *hidden-data spaces* defined by the algorithm designer. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Parameter estimation of superimposed signals using the EM algorithm (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/3175245_Parameter_estimation_of_superimposed_signals_using_the_EM_algorithm)\nciteturn659search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Parameter estimation of superimposed signals using the EM algorithm ... Download full-text PDF Read full-text ... A computationally efficient algorithm for parameter estimation of superimposed signals based on the two-step iterative EM (estimate-and-maximize, with 